    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Madrid began as a walled military outpost built by the emir Muhammad I in the late 9th century, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1083 or 1085, and grew into a middle-ranking town of the Crown of Castile.</li><li>Philip II set his court there in June 1561, and from then Madrid was the seat of the monarchy, except between 1601 and 1606, when the court sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> and the population fell.</li><li>The city rose against French troops on 2 May 1808, was besieged from October 1936 in the Civil War until it fell on 28 March 1939, and was confirmed as the capital by the constitutions of 1931 and 1978.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When the court went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> in 1601, Madrid reportedly lost half of its people, and it was won back in 1606 partly by an offer of 250,000 ducats from the town to help pay for the court’s return.</p></aside>
    </section>

