    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">9th century</span><span class="rf-text">The emir Muhammad I has a citadel built on the left bank of the Manzanares</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1083</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> takes Madrid, though some historians date the conquest to 1085</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1561</span><span class="rf-text">Philip II settles his court in Madrid</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1601</span><span class="rf-text">The court moves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> and Madrid loses much of its population; it returns in 1606</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1808</span><span class="rf-text">The people of Madrid rise against the French on 2 May</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1858</span><span class="rf-text">The Canal de Isabel II brings the waters of the Lozoya to the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">The military rising is defeated in Madrid and the city is besieged until 1939</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1978</span><span class="rf-text">The new constitution confirms Madrid as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Madrid lies on the left bank of the Manzanares, on the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta Central</a> in the middle of the Iberian peninsula, about 60 kilometres south of the Guadarrama mountains. The site has been occupied since prehistoric times. Archaeological remains include a Celtic Carpetani settlement, Roman villas, a Visigoth basilica near the church of Santa María de la Almudena and three Visigoth necropolises near Casa de Campo, Tetuán and Vicálvaro. The origin of the name is unknown. Proposed derivations include a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> form from matrix, a Celtic form with a root meaning “ford”, and the Arabic majrā or majrīṭ, a word for a water stream or spring.</p><p>The first historical document of an established settlement dates from the Muslim period. In the second half of the 9th century the Umayyad emir Muhammad I built a fortress on a headland near the Manzanares, one of many he ordered on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> and the kingdoms of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. It was meant to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> from Christian attacks from the north and to serve as a starting point for Muslim offensives. Its building is dated to between 852 and 886. The Muslim town, Majriṭ, consisted of the alcázar and a wider walled citadel, with some housing outside the walls, and it was largely a military outpost where troops were quartered. Local power was held by the Banū Sālim until the proclamation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>. The most generous estimate for the 10th century, put forward tentatively, is 2,000 inhabitants. After the Caliphate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> broke up in the early 11th century, Madrid was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of Toledo.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega.jpg" alt="Lope de Vega" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega">Lope de Vega</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LopedeVega.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Eugenio Caxés, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Christian conquest Madrid became a town of middle rank in the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Before the 15th century its society rested on agriculture, with a noteworthy number of irrigated crops, and the making of building materials and of leather were its two important industries. In the 15th century the Trastámara kings favoured it. The alcázar often kept the royal treasure, the town had a mint from 1467 to 1471, and Henry IV spent more time there than anywhere else and died there. By the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it held one of the 18 votes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-castile">Cortes of Castile</a> and had many hermitages and hospitals.</p><p>In June 1561 Philip II set his court in Madrid, in the old alcázar, and the city became the political centre of the monarchy. The court drew leading writers and artists, among them Cervantes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega">Lope de Vega</a> and Velázquez, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-golden-age">Siglo de Oro</a>, and the early 17th century was a heyday for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in the corrales de comedias. The Plaza Mayor was built in 1617 to 1619 and the Buen Retiro park was founded in 1631. The city lived by supplying its own growing population, including the royal household and government, and by banking and publishing, so that it was more a place of consumption than of production or trade. Philip V built the Royal Palace and the main Royal Academies, and Charles III built or pushed forward the Prado, the Puerta de Alcalá and the Royal Observatory. Large-scale industry came only in the 20th century, especially around the 1960s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile.jpg" alt="Alfonso VI of Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlfonsoVI_of_Castile.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploaded by MaiDireLollo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Madrid was built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a> and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> broke up, belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> seized it in 1083 as an outpost against Toledo, which he took in 1085; historians debate whether Madrid fell before Toledo or as a result of its fall. Alfonso VII confirmed it as a villa de realengo, linked to the Crown, in 1123. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> gave it its first charter for the municipal council in 1202, Ferdinand III expanded it in 1222, and in 1346 Alfonso XI changed the government of the town to a regimiento of 12 regidores. In 1383 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-castile">John I of Castile</a> gave the lordship of Madrid to Leo V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and the council made sure that the privilege did not become hereditary.</p><p>Madrid joined the revolt of the Comuneros against Charles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and after the defeat at Villalar it was besieged and occupied by imperial troops, though it received the titles Coronada and Imperial. From 1561 it was the seat of the Hispanic Monarchy, except for the court’s years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> from 1601 to 1606. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> it changed hands several times, and Philip V entered it on 3 December 1710. In 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> to abdicate and made his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a> king. After the Peninsular War Ferdinand VII returned in 1814, and the administrative reforms of 1833 made Madrid the capital of the new liberal state under Isabella II. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1868 deposed her. The Constitution of 1931 was the first to name Madrid as the capital, and the constitution of 1978 confirmed it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Muslim rule Madrid was a garrison town. After the conquest the Christians occupied the centre and the Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were displaced to the suburbs, and the mosque was reconsecrated as the church of the Virgin of Almudena. The population included Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarabs</a>, Castilian-Leonese settlers, a minority of influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and a Jewish community that was probably smaller than the mudéjar one and stood out for its physicians. By the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the best-placed mudéjars were the alarifes, master builders who were responsible for public works and had a leading role in the town’s urbanism in the 15th century. Facing the decree of expulsion of 1492 few Jews left and most converted, and after the law of forced conversion of 1502 many mudéjars adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, which the council encouraged with tax benefits and gifts.</p><p>In the 17th century Madrid had a society of estates. The nobility and the high clergy, who were also of noble origin, stood at the top and swarmed around the court. There were many civil servants, a small number of craftsmen and traders, and at the bottom homeless people and unemployed immigrants. At the end of the Ancien Régime the city held a slave population tentatively estimated at 6,000 to 15,000 of a total over 150,000. Water came through the viajes de agua, channels built in the 17th century, until the Canal de Isabel II was inaugurated on 24 June 1858. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-alcala">University of Alcalá</a> de Henares was moved to Madrid in 1836 and became the Central University,.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Henry III rebuilt the town after a fire had destroyed it. During the 1601 to 1606 stay of the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> the population reportedly fell by half, and an estimated 50,000 to 60,000 people left. In March 1766 riots broke out, the Esquilache Riots, against the reforms of Charles III’s Sicilian minister and a decree banning the long cloak and wide-brimmed hat, at a time of famine and rising prices. On 2 May 1808 a crowd gathered at the Royal Palace as French soldiers removed members of the royal family; the French fired into the crowd, fighting lasted for hours, and the artillery barracks at Monteleón resisted, where Luis Daoíz and Pedro Velarde died. Hundreds were killed. In the student unrest of 10 April 1865 there were 14 deaths, 74 wounded students and 114 arrests.</p><p>In July 1936 the military rising was defeated in Madrid by loyal forces and workers’ militias, after the storming of the Cuartel de la Montaña on 20 July. Madrid was bombed from the air from the night of 27 to 28 August 1936, besieged from October, and the scene of a major battle in its western suburbs in November. In the Republican city, killings of prisoners in the summer and autumn included the Paracuellos massacres. In March 1939 a coup by Colonel Casado against the government led to fighting that left roughly 2,000 casualties, and the city fell to the nationalists on 28 March 1939. Post-war rationing and scarcity caused high mortality from malnutrition, and by 1956 as many as 50,000 shacks stood around the city. On 11 March 2004 bombs on trains killed 191 people and injured 1,800.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii.jpg" alt="Alfonso XIII" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rey_Alfonso_XIII_de_Espa%C3%B1a,_by_Kaulak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kaulak, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Royal Palace, the Prado, the Puerta de Alcalá, the Plaza Mayor and the Paseo del Prado come from the reigns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. The Gran Vía, a thoroughfare cut through the old centre with the demolition of many houses and small streets, was begun in April 1910 at a ceremony led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a>, and the Metro opened its first line, from Sol to Cuatro Caminos, in 1919. The Prado, the Reina Sofía and the Thyssen-Bornemisza museums stand along the Paseo del Prado. Goya’s painting The Second of May 1808 records the uprising. The Royal Spanish Academy, whose motto is “Limpia, fija y da resplendor”, governs the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-language">Spanish language</a> from Madrid, and the National Library holds the largest collection of any public library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>The city grew by annexing 13 neighbouring municipalities between 1948 and 1954, which raised its area from 68.42 to 607.09 square kilometres. Its population peaked in 1975 at 3,228,057, then fell, and recovered to roughly 3 million by the end of the 20th century. From the late 1970s the cultural movement known as la Movida was centred there. The city keeps the feast of its patron, Saint Isidore the Laborer, on 15 May, and the day of the 1808 uprising, 2 May, is the regional holiday. Madrid is today the capital of Spain and of the Community of Madrid, and the city proper has over 3.4 million people.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria (Canticles of Holy Mary)<span>1275</span></a><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego<span>1820</span></a><a href="/songs/la-paloma">La Paloma<span>c. 1860</span></a><a href="/songs/einheitsfrontlied">Einheitsfrontlied<span>1934</span></a><a href="/songs/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol<span>1935</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Madrid began as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> fortress on the Manzanares in the 9th century and was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> in 1083 or 1085. Philip II made it the seat of the court in 1561. It rose against the French in 1808, withstood a siege in the Civil War and is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</em></p>

